      "Abstract": {
        "CopyrightInformation": "© 2017 Society for Applied Microbiology and John Wiley & Sons Ltd.",
        "AbstractText": "Solar panels located on high (Arctic and Antarctic) latitudes combine the harshness of the climate with that of the solar exposure. We report here that these polar solar panels are inhabited by similar microbial communities in taxonomic terms, dominated by Hymenobacter spp., Sphingomonas spp. and Ascomycota. Our results suggest that solar panels, even on high latitudes, can shape a microbial ecosystem adapted to irradiation and desiccation."
      },
